<div><h3>I. Introduction</h3><p>Children in low resourced environments (LREs) such as conflict and post-conflict zones and low to middle income countries have limited access to appropriate prosthetic devices to return functionality after limb loss [1]. This is especially pertinent for children with above knee amputation who require a prosthetic knee joint at as early an age as possible to allow for successful adoption of appropriate gait patterns [2]. In these LREs the most widely available device is the polypropylene technology delivered by the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) [3], [4]. Although this technology is currently providing an effective basic solution to these children, the device was designed in the previous century and lacks features that are now deemed essential in mechanical prosthetic knee joints for successful non-disabled gait replication. These essential features include a swing phase control mechanism (SPCM).</p></div>